&+ychannel of salt-crusted timber, barely wide enough for a single
&+Lwarrior to pass. To either side, the massive rowing benches
&+Lare worn smooth and dark by the friction of generations of
&+ystraining bodies. The air here is trapped and heavy, thick with
&+Lthe rhythmic, guttural grunts of the rowers and the metallic
&+Ltang of the heavy iron chains that secure the oars to the hull.
